    <DataNode elementId="AT1G30620-MONOMER" textLabel="UDP-arabinose 4-epimerase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9SA77" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="900" centerY="695" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">&lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; plants defective in expression of the &lt;i&gt;MUR4&lt;/i&gt; gene display a 50% reduction in the monosaccharide L-arabinose in most organs and affects arabinose-containing pectic cell wall polysaccharides and arabinogalactan proteins |CITS:[10517829]|. The defect was shown to affect the &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; synthesis of activated UDP-L-arabinose from UDP-D-xylose. The protein purified from yeast &lt;i&gt;Pichia pastoris&lt;/i&gt; extracts displays UDP-D-xylose 4-epimerase activity |CITS:[12566589]|. Interestingly, little difference in UDP-D-Xyl 4-epimerase activity could be detected between microsomal protein fractions of mutant &lt;i&gt;mur4&lt;/i&gt; and those of wild-type &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; |CITS:[10517829]|. This result was attributed to genetic redundancy |CITS:[10517829]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT1G30620-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT1G30620.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="Q9SA77" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Pid" value="AAO39213" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-69" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT1G30620.1" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT1G30620" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_15134748" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_10517829" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_12566589" />
    </DataNode>
    <DataNode elementId="AT1G53500-MONOMER" textLabel="AT1G53500-MONOMER" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9LPG6" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="775" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">&lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; plants deficient in MUM4 only contain 35 to 45% of the wild type-content of rhamnose in their seed mucilage |CITS:[14671019]|. This enzyme, the gene of which has very high sequence similarity to bacterial rhamnose synthesis genes, is postulated to have UDP-4-keto-6-deoxy-D-glucose 3,5-epimerase-4-reductase activity and can catalyze the conversion of UDP-D-glucose into UDP-L-rhamnose. This observation is corroborated by the heterologous expression of a MUM4 paralog, RHM1, in &lt;i&gt;E.coli&lt;/i&gt; which resulted in an enzyme fraction which converts UDP-D-glucose into a product that comigrates with rhamnose |CITS:[14671019]|. The enzyme has not been purified at the time of this review and further analysis is required to confirm its catalytic activity. MUM4 has two paralogs in &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt;: RHM1 and RHM3.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT1G53500-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT1G53500.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="Q9LPG6" />
